use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Selection import Selection
from ../../../behaviors/Traversal import Traversal
from ../../../behaviors/Virtualisation import Virtualisation
from ../../fields/TextField import TextField
from ../../toggles/Checkbox import Checkbox

export ui-component TreeView:
    description:
        A tree view shows items nested inside other items, like folders and
        files. Items that hold others are branches, and the user can open and
        close them to show or hide what's inside. The user can move through it
        with the keyboard and, if the component allows it, pick items.

    composes:
        - @{Checkbox}
        - @{TextField}

    behaviors:
        - @{Traversal}
        - @{Selection}
        - @{Virtualisation}

    items:
        Each item has a name that assistive tools can read out. Assistive
        tools are also told how deep it is, its position among the items
        beside it, and, for a branch, whether it is open. How deep an item is
        is shown on screen too, usually by indenting it. What else an item
        shows, such as an icon, is up to the component using the tree.

    opening:
        Pressing a branch's open-and-close control opens or closes it. The
        component decides whether pressing anywhere on the branch does the
        same. Closing a branch remembers which branches inside it were open, so
        they are open again when it reopens. If focus was inside a branch that
        closes, it moves to that branch.

    keyboard:
        Up and Down move through the items that can be seen, passing over
        anything inside a closed branch. Right opens a closed branch, or moves
        into an open one. Left closes an open branch, or moves out to the
        branch holding the current item. Home and End go to the first and last
        item that can be seen, without opening or closing anything. A key such
        as the asterisk opens every branch beside the current one; which key is
        up to the chosen library.

    typeAhead:
        Typing letters jumps to the next item that can be seen whose name
        starts with what was typed. Items inside closed branches are not
        searched.

    picking:
        The component decides whether the tree lets the user pick nothing, one
        item, or several, as Selection describes. Branches can be picked like
        any other item. Picking a branch doesn't pick what's inside it.

    checking:
        The component can give each item a Checkbox instead, as a separate
        thing from picking. Checking a branch checks everything inside it,
        and a branch where only some of what's inside is checked shows as
        partly checked.

    loadingOnDemand:
        A branch's contents can be fetched the first time it is opened,
        instead of all at once. While they load, the branch shows that it is
        busy. The component can say how many items a branch will have before
        they arrive. If loading fails, the branch stays closed, the component
        is told, and opening it again tries again.

    renaming:
        The component can let the user rename items in place. A key such as
        F2 turns the item's name into a TextField. Enter or moving away saves
        the new name, and Escape puts back the old one. Spaces at either end
        are trimmed, and a blank name is refused. The component decides which
        items can be renamed, can refuse a new name, and is told the name that
        was saved.

    disabled:
        Disabled items are shown but can't be picked, opened, or renamed.

    long:
        Virtualisation is only switched on for trees long enough to need it.

    nonGoals:
        - No dragging items to move them between branches
        - No columns. Nested rows of data with column headings are a tree table
        - No searching that hides items. Typing only moves focus
